Yes. A role becomes easier to inhabit when the environment is built to support it.

A king without a court is just a person making claims. A player without an arena has nowhere for skill to become legible. So you can deliberately construct the surrounding system:

role
→ court / arena
→ rituals
→ symbols
→ audience
→ feedback
→ repeated enactment
→ identity becomes easier to sustain

That applies to careers, athletics, leadership, art, business, even personal habits. You can choose clothes, spaces, routines, collaborators, language, tools, and public commitments that make the version of yourself you are trying to build easier to express.

The useful distinction is between scaffolding and counterfeiting.

Scaffolding says:

build an environment that helps the desired qualities become real

Counterfeiting says:

build an environment that merely makes those qualities appear real

The strongest version aligns both:

desired image
≈ actual behavior
≈ environmental reinforcement
≈ long-term plan

Then the court is not just decoration. It becomes part of the machinery producing the person.

I’d call that Role Ecology:

Don’t only choose the player. Build the arena in which that player naturally emerges.